The &lt;% ... %&gt; block (scriptlet) contains arbitrary Java code; the &lt;%= ... %&gt; block (expression) inserts the value of a Java expression directly into the generated HTML. It is exactly this userName attribute, placed in the request by the Servlet through request.setAttribute(...), that the JSP retrieves here.

5.8 Scopes: Sharing Information Within the Server
Just as Servlets can share information with other modules through attributes (see Chapter 4), those attributes always exist within a given scope. There are four kinds of scope:

Web context (or application context): a single one per application, shared by all modules and all users
Session: one per user, kept across several requests from that user
Request: one per request, lost as soon as the response is sent
Page: exclusive to JSP, not used in this course

All scopes follow the same interface: setAttribute/getAttribute methods, and getAttributeNames() to list everything stored there.
Web context, shared by the whole application:
ServletContext context = request.getServletContext();
context.setAttribute("dbHost", "localhost");
String dbHost = (String) context.getAttribute("dbHost");
Session, already introduced, shared only by the same user:
HttpSession session = request.getSession();
session.setAttribute("email", "foo@fool.com");
String email = (String) session.getAttribute("email");
Request, the most restricted of all, shared only between modules processing the same request (for example, between a Servlet and the JSP it forwards to):
request.setAttribute("email", "foo@fool.com");
String email = (String) request.getAttribute("email");
The four scopes form an inclusion hierarchy, from the widest to the most restricted: Application contains Session, which contains Request, which contains Page. The wider the scope, the more widely shared the information, and the greater the responsibility to manage it correctly (for instance, to remove it when it is no longer needed).






Figure 5.2: The four scopes, from the widest (Application) to the most restricted (Page): each contains the next, and a wider scope means more sharing, but also more responsibility in managing it.









Scopes are not instantiated



Scope objects (ServletContext, HttpSession, ServletRequest) are automatically provided by the container. A new instance is never created with new: if one does not yet exist, the appropriate methods (getServletContext(), getSession()) create it or return the existing one.

6.2 Authentication vs. Authorisation
Before solving the problem, it is worth pinning down two often-confused concepts:

Authentication

Answers “who is the user?”. It is the identity check, typically performed at login time.


if (username.equals("admin") && password.equals("1234")) {
    HttpSession session = request.getSession(true);
    session.setAttribute("UserName", username);
}

Authorisation

Answers “what is this user allowed to access?”. It is the permission check, performed once identity has already been confirmed.


if (session.getAttribute("UserRole").equals("ADMIN")) {
    // access granted
}
Authentication always happens first: it makes no sense to check the permissions of someone whose identity has not yet been confirmed. This distinction is directly reflected in the HTTP status codes used in the response (see Chapter 3):

401 Unauthorized: the request is not authenticated
403 Forbidden: the request is authenticated, but lacks permission for the requested resource

6.7 Declaring and Implementing a Filter
A Filter is declared with the @WebFilter annotation, indicating the URL pattern it applies to:
@WebFilter("/*")
public class AuthenticationFilter implements Filter {
    // ...
}
The pattern "/*" intercepts every request, with no exception. The class must implement jakarta.servlet.Filter, and the container discovers it automatically at deployment time. Just like Servlets, a Filter can also be declared equivalently in the web.xml file, although this practice is no longer much in use.
A typical doFilter(), protecting every request by requiring a valid session, has this form:
public void doFilter(ServletRequest request,
                      ServletResponse response,
                      FilterChain chain)
        throws IOException, ServletException {

    HttpServletRequest req = (HttpServletRequest) request;
    HttpServletResponse res = (HttpServletResponse) response;

    HttpSession session = req.getSession(false);

    if (session == null || session.getAttribute("UserName") == null) {
        res.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED); // 401
        return;
    }

    chain.doFilter(request, response);
}
In practice, not every request should be protected: /login itself, and static resources such as /css, /js or /images, need to remain accessible without authentication — otherwise the user could never even reach the login form.
String path = req.getRequestURI();

boolean isLogin = path.endsWith("/login");
boolean isStatic = path.startsWith(req.getContextPath() + "/css")
                 || path.startsWith(req.getContextPath() + "/js")
                 || path.startsWith(req.getContextPath() + "/images");

if (isLogin || isStatic) {
    chain.doFilter(request, response);
    return;
}

8.4 The Lifecycle of a Token
A token does not replace the moment of authentication itself — it only replaces the way the server remembers it.

Issuance

The client authenticates exactly as in Chapter 5, sending user/pass to a login endpoint. The server validates the credentials (for example, through a DAO, see Chapter 4) but, instead of creating an HttpSession, generates a new JWT, signs it with its secret key, and returns it to the client in the response body

Storage and resending

Unlike JSESSIONID (see Chapter 5), which the browser resends automatically through a cookie, the client is responsible for storing the received token and for explicitly resending it in the Authorization header of every subsequent request

Validation

Each request is validated completely independently of previous ones: the server recomputes the signature, without consulting any session at all (see Section 8.5)

Expiry

The exp claim in the payload defines how long the token remains valid. Once that moment has passed, the server rejects it (401) even if the signature is still correct, forcing a new login. This limits the time window in which an intercepted token could be misused — unlike an HttpSession, which only ends through explicit logout or prolonged inactivity (see Chapter 5), a JWT always expires, even if it is still being used


The following table summarises the difference between the two authentication models seen so far in this course:









Session (HttpSession)
JWT




Where the state lives
On the server, in memory
In the token itself, on the client side


What the client keeps
An identifier (JSESSIONID)
The full token, already carrying the data


Validation on each request
A lookup in the server’s memory
Signature verification, with no lookup at all


Multiple server instances
Need to share the session with one another
Each validates independently



In practice, a Filter (see Chapter 6) remains the natural place for this validation to happen. Recall the session-based authentication Filter already presented in that chapter:
public void doFilter(ServletRequest request, ServletResponse response, FilterChain chain)
        throws IOException, ServletException {

    HttpServletRequest req = (HttpServletRequest) request;
    HttpServletResponse res = (HttpServletResponse) response;

    HttpSession session = req.getSession(false);

    if (session == null || session.getAttribute("UserName") == null) {
        res.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED); // 401
        return;
    }

    chain.doFilter(request, response);
}
The equivalent, token-based version has exactly the same structure — only the check itself changes, from an HttpSession lookup to validating the token’s signature and validity. That validation is rarely written from scratch: it is sensitive code, involving cryptography, and it pays off to leave it to a well-tested, actively maintained library, rather than reimplementing it. JJWT is one of the most widely used in Java, and one of the more security-conscious in the ecosystem (for example, it requires the expected algorithm to be stated explicitly, to prevent a common attack in which a token with no signature at all would be accepted as valid):
public void doFilter(ServletRequest request, ServletResponse response, FilterChain chain)
        throws IOException, ServletException {

    HttpServletRequest req = (HttpServletRequest) request;
    HttpServletResponse res = (HttpServletResponse) response;

    String authHeader = req.getHeader("Authorization");

    if (authHeader == null || !authHeader.startsWith("Bearer ")) {
        res.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED); // 401
        return;
    }

    String token = authHeader.substring("Bearer ".length());

    try {
        Claims claims = Jwts.parser()
                .verifyWith(SECRET_KEY)          // the server's secret key
                .build()
                .parseSignedClaims(token)
                .getPayload();

        req.setAttribute("UserName", claims.getSubject());
    } catch (JwtException e) {
        res.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED); // invalid signature or expired token
        return;
    }

    chain.doFilter(request, response);
}
The call to parseSignedClaims(token) internally does everything the validation requires: it decodes the header and the payload, recomputes the signature with SECRET_KEY and compares it with the one received, and checks the exp claim — throwing a JwtException (or one of its subclasses, such as ExpiredJwtException for an expired token, or SignatureException for an invalid signature) should any of these checks fail. Note the structural similarity to the session-based Filter: in both cases, a failure condition ends the request with 401, and the chain only continues with chain.doFilter() if authentication is valid.

8.7 Consuming a REST API in JavaScript: fetch() and JSON
The Fetch API is the standard way for a browser to issue HTTP requests from JavaScript, without reloading the page. A simple, unauthenticated request looks like this:
fetch("/country/PT")
  .then(response =&gt; response.json())
  .then(data =&gt; {
    console.log(data.capital);
  });
fetch() returns a promise — a value that is not yet available, but will be, later, once the request finishes. The first .then() receives the raw HTTP response, and converts its body, as JSON text, into a plain JavaScript object through response.json() — this step, in turn, also returns a promise, so it is chained with a second .then(), with the data now ready to use.
For an authenticated request (see Section 8.2 and Section 8.3), the token travels in a header, just like any other authenticated HTTP request:
fetch("/country", {
  headers: { "Authorization": "Bearer " + token }
})
  .then(response =&gt; response.json())
  .then(data =&gt; {
    // ...
  });
A POST request, creating a resource, carries its body already in JSON, explicitly stating that format in the Content-Type header (see Chapter 4):
fetch("/country", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ code: "PT", name: "Portugal", capital: "Lisboa" })
})
  .then(response =&gt; response.json())
  .then(data =&gt; {
    // ...
  });

8.8 The DOM as the Browser’s “View”
The DOM (Document Object Model) is the in-memory representation of the HTML page the browser is displaying — a tree of objects, one per page element, that JavaScript can read and change directly. It is through the DOM that JavaScript plays the role of View (see Section 8.6): it does not generate HTML as text, the way a JSP does (see Chapter 5), but instead locates elements already present on the page and updates their content.
fetch("/country/PT")
  .then(response =&gt; response.json())
  .then(data =&gt; {
    document.querySelector("#capital").textContent = data.capital;
  });
document.querySelector(selector) locates the first page element matching the given selector (here, the element with id="capital"), returning a reference to that DOM element; textContent is the property that sets the text shown inside it. The same principle applies to a list of results: for each item returned by the API, a new HTML element is created and inserted into the page.
fetch("/country")
  .then(response =&gt; response.json())
  .then(countries =&gt; {
    const table = document.querySelector("#tabela-paises");
    countries.forEach(country =&gt; {
      const row = document.createElement("tr");
      row.innerHTML = `&lt;td&gt;${country.code}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;${country.name}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;${country.capital}&lt;/td&gt;`;
      table.appendChild(row);
    });
  });
This pattern — requesting data from a REST API with fetch(), and using the DOM to display it — is the foundation of any JavaScript front-office connected to a REST service, regardless of the specific application or data involved.

